  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Initial Access", "technique": "T1078.004", "what_happened": "At 2026-09-22 02:47:11 MYT, AzureAD SignIn for cloud.storageadmin@corp.local from external IP 91.236.116.78 (geolocation: Bucharest, Romania), deviceDetail=Unknown, appDisplayName='Azure Storage Explorer' / Microsoft.Azure.Storage user agent. resultType=0 (success). The account's baseline location is Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia; never previously seen from Romania. MFA satisfied via long-lived refresh token (no interactive prompt). The token was likely stolen via earlier session-cookie exfil or AAD Connect compromise (not directly observed in this alert window).", "chain_reason": "AzureAD audit (Wazuh azure-logs module): rule 91540 SignIn with data.azure.identity=cloud.storageadmin@corp.local, data.azure.ipAddress=91.236.116.78, data.azure.location='Bucharest, Romania', data.azure.deviceDetail='Unknown', data.azure.appDisplayName='Azure Storage Explorer'. Anomalous-geo SignIn for a privileged storage owner account = T1078.004 Cloud Account abuse.", "first_seen": "2026-09-22T02:47:11+0800"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Impact / Inhibit Recovery", "technique": "T1490", "what_happened": "From 02:48:33 onward, mass DeleteBlob burst against storage account 'corpprodassets01'. Wazuh aggregated rule (rule 92800) fired 'High-volume blob delete anomaly: 4,217 DeleteBlob operations in 53 seconds from a single identity'. Operations span containers 'production-data-prod' (2,891 blobs), 'customer-files' (964 blobs), and 'backups-prod' (362 blobs — note: backup container hit DELIBERATELY to inhibit recovery). All operations carry data.azure.identity=cloud.storageadmin@corp.local + data.azure.ipAddress=91.236.116.78. Critical observation: blob versioning was enabled on production-data-prod and customer-files BUT NOT on backups-prod (control gap). The blobs in versioned containers are soft-deleted (recoverable); the blobs in backups-prod are permanently lost unless rehydrated from secondary geo-replicated backup.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh aggregated rule 92800 (volume-anomaly: >1000 DeleteBlob from one identity within 60s); underlying events have data.azure.operation='DeleteBlob' with data.azure.resourceUri pattern '/subscriptions/<id>/resourceGroups/.../storageAccounts/corpprodassets01/blobServices/default/containers/<name>/blobs/<name>'. 4,217 operations in 53s = scripted automation (Azure Storage REST API or AzCopy), not interactive admin. T1485 Data Destruction + T1490 Inhibit System Recovery (backup-container targeting).", "first_seen": "2026-09-22T02:48:33+0800"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Impact / Ransom Note Drop", "technique": "T1486", "what_happened": "At 03:02:18 MYT, immediately after the delete burst, 47 PutBlob operations created an identical 2.1 KB blob named '_README_RESTORE.txt' at the root of every container in the storage account (one note per container, including admin-internal containers like 'logs-archive' and 'iac-state'). The blob content (recovered via storage diagnostic Capture by the Azure team during this investigation) reads as a standard ransom demand: 0.5 BTC payment within 72h to a wallet address bc1q... with a Tor onion contact URL. Same source identity cloud.storageadmin@corp.local + same IP 91.236.116.78.", "chain_reason": "Azure Storage events: data.azure.operation='PutBlob', data.azure.objectId pattern matching '*/_README_RESTORE.txt' across 47 distinct containers within 38 seconds, all from same identity + IP. The ransom-note-as-blob pattern is the canonical T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact signature for cloud-native ransomware (no binary required — APIs do the destruction, the note announces it).", "first_seen": "2026-09-22T03:02:18+0800"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Defense Evasion / Access Manipulation", "technique": "T1531", "what_happened": "At 03:09:42 MYT, attacker performed SetContainerACL on 'customer-files' setting public-blob access (anonymous read), AND issued a new Storage Account access key via 'List Keys' + 'Regenerate Key' Azure Resource Manager operations on 'corpprodassets01'. The new access key (the Azure-generated 88-char base64 key) becomes the long-term backdoor — independent of cloud.storageadmin's identity, surviving any session revocation or password reset on the user account. The public-ACL change on customer-files is the data-leak threat lever: the attacker can publish the ransom message externally and threaten to make customer data world-readable if ransom is not paid.", "chain_reason": "Azure ResourceManager + Storage events: data.azure.operation='SetContainerACL' with data.azure.objectId='customer-files' and publicAccess='blob'; followed by data.azure.operation='Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/listKeys/action' and data.azure.operation='Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/regenerateKey/action' on the storage account. T1531 Account Access Removal (legitimate identity owner is being LOCKED OUT by key rotation favoring attacker key) + T1098 Account Manipulation.", "first_seen": "2026-09-22T03:09:42+0800"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Persistence / Exfiltration Channel", "technique": "T1537", "what_happened": "At 03:13:08 MYT, ListBlob enumeration + GetBlob downloads from 91.236.116.78 against 'customer-files' container — 312 GB transferred outbound in the final 56 seconds of the session (storage account egress metrics confirm). Attacker dual-purposed the storage compromise: not just destruction (mass delete) but also DATA THEFT (download customer-files content to attacker infra) — classic double-extortion ransomware playbook applied to cloud-native object storage.", "chain_reason": "Azure Storage events: data.azure.operation='ListBlob' on customer-files then a burst of data.azure.operation='GetBlob' with cumulative response-size metrics matching 312 GB over the 56s window; data.azure.ipAddress=91.236.116.78 throughout. T1537 Transfer Data to Cloud Account + T1485 + T1486 = double-extortion pattern.", "first_seen": "2026-09-22T03:13:08+0800"}
  ],
